mdunitdunit‚dmarkf200000ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;H6RnrDJEyfPK12OALGp8vIFH_EeuEr5ap5M9p2OYs_cƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarki0.4198908ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;LreRx1DnyxYzHHeujguS8sdUgINqlfNV4SJ0_gMichkƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka4ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;C6lHVsQIrnlarfYW5EQ8fMGZd0SzMaA2nNf3Shc_wokƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarke32000ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;6dfVoui7nsSIyqQvCUEbgu6RhuNpKzmDBK6idkpvdAkƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkd9294ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;3zkcgNKM_9ABfg87VAlMb5sEN4t8y6oRgvz-UyLKKloƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka0ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;5CkQ-E8DlJB69x-bdFjelolOOOe1xcQc8lxAIiCx0-0ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka4ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;7z7tZ_C2S-UNYar5azbadY5lqSx1tnFI6I2LJDYD28wƒdpairƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;lPRijp2q38nM0LZS16Hz-vaqrtHO4UJCQMBYyZdgNVcƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarky&{"tool":"submit_revision_request","failureLayer":"writer-model","replayFrom":"Add missing inline citations to the final showdown section and Chicago Bulls paragraph, then resubmit for review.","summary":"The manuscript is a structurally sound pre-match analysis with good use of the primary source in the first half, but the second major section drops inline attribution for six or more factual claims drawn from Context 1, and the Chicago Bulls paragraph in the first section is also uncited. Both defects are bounded writer-model failures repairable without replaying research or reassigning the story.","requiredRepairs":["Add inline citations (linking to https://theconversation.com/tactically-complete-spain-vs-an-inspired-messi-led-argentina-who-will-win-the-world-cup-final-287372) for every factual claim in 'The geometry of the final showdown' section that derives from Context 1, specifically: Spain conceding only one goal throughout the tournament; six different players scoring for Spain; Messi's energy-conserving role enabled by ten teammates carrying the physical burden; the 'street fight' framing attributed to Rodri/Argentina's approach; Spain's pursuit of a second title following 2010; Argentina pursuing a fourth title and back-to-back championships not achieved in six decades.","Add a citation to the primary source [Geometry, football and the World Cup final](https://theconversation.com/geometry-football-and-the-world-cup-final-287792) in the Chicago Bulls/Phil Jackson paragraph inside 'The geometry of football triangles' â€” those claims (Phil Jackson era 1987-1998, Tex Winter's triangle offence, triangles preserve alternatives) originate in the primary source and the paragraph carries no inline link.","Resolve the deterministic modality warning: the description/summary phrase 'they approach the contest with fundamentally different tactical philosophies' is an unattributed interpretive claim; attach explicit attribution to Context 1 or soften to acknowledged interpretation."],"report":"## Editorial Assessment â€” geometry-football-and-the-world-cup-final\n\n### Deterministic standards\nThe automated gate returned `publishable: true` with one `warning` (attention-preserve-modality) flagging the description/summary for certainty language on an interpretive claim. That warning is addressed in repair item 3 below. No blocking gate failures are active.\n\n### Source and assignment adequacy\nThe primary source (Conor Boland, The Conversation, 2026-07-17) is a well-evidenced academic analysis of Spain's passing geometry, with specific match events (Porro's 58th-minute goal, the MbappÃ© post-match quote via Reuters), player-tracking research citations, and the Chicago Bulls analogy â€” all material that can support the assigned article. Context 1 (Steve Georgakis, The Conversation, 2026-07-17) provides adequately specific claims about the two squads (goals conceded, scorers, Rodri's role, historical title context). The assignment is well-matched to the available evidence. No reporting-needed condition applies.\n\n### Headline and lede\nThe headline \"Spain's Geometry Meets Messi's Magic in World Cup Final Showdown\" captures both analytical threads (Spain's geometric system, Argentina's Messi-centric structure) and adds \"Showdown\" for register without fabricating conflict not in the sources. It preserves the primary source's organizing terms. Acceptable.\n\nThe lede names the event concretely (who, where, what), cites both key sources before making factual claims, and correctly attributes the \"fitting finale between the world's two highest-ranked teams\" framing to Context 1 via the \"according to\" construction. Slightly awkward phrasing but not materially defective; the citation is visible.\n\n### Body â€” first section and \"The geometry of football triangles\"\nThe first body paragraph accurately synthesizes the primary source: passing triangles, the choice-forcing mechanism, France's failure to close passing options, and MbappÃ©'s attributed quote. The MbappÃ© quotation is hyperlinked to the Reuters URL embedded within the primary source â€” the attribution chain is visible even if the manuscript cites the sub-link rather than the primary source itself. Acceptable.\n\n**Material defect:** The Chicago Bulls/Phil Jackson paragraph contains four distinct factual claims originating in the primary source (Phil Jackson's 1987â€“1998 tenure, Tex Winter's triangle offence, the mechanics of placing three players on one side of the court, and the principle that triangles preserve alternatives). No inline citation appears in this paragraph. Under the article's own writing law â€” \"every factual paragraph must make its reporting source unambiguous through an inline source link or an immediately continuing attribution\" â€” this is a clear defect.\n\nThe remainder of this section (the probability-map discussion, the Porro/Olmo goal) is adequately tied to the primary source via the opening link.\n\n### Body â€” \"The geometry of the final showdown\"\nThis section draws entirely on Context 1 for its factual substance: Spain's single goal conceded, the six-player scoring distribution, Messi's energy-conserving role, the \"street fight\" framing tied to Rodri, the 2010 title reference, and the six-decades claim on back-to-back championships. **None of these paragraphs carry an inline citation.** The attribution requirement is violated across the full section. This is the most material defect in the manuscript.\n\nThe closing paragraphs (\"As these approaches collideâ€¦\") are analytical extensions using appropriately hedged language (\"could determine,\" \"might allow\"). They do not introduce invented facts. However, because the preceding paragraphs lack attribution, the analytical extension sits on an uncited factual base, compounding the problem.\n\n### Modality and certainty\nThe description/summary phrase \"they approach the contest with fundamentally different tactical philosophies\" presents an interpretive characterization as settled fact without visible attribution, reproducing the deterministic gate's modality warning. Repairable by attaching attribution to Context 1 or hedging the phrasing.\n\n### Fairness and harm\nNo allegations of wrongdoing, no named individuals criticized, no disputed claims requiring right-of-reply. No stereotyping, sensationalism, or commercial influence detected. Clean on fairness grounds.\n\n### Pipeline diagnosis\nSources and assignment are adequate. The instructions clearly required inline citations for every factual paragraph. The writer model maintained citation discipline in the first section but abandoned it entirely in the second named section and dropped it for one paragraph in the first. This is a writer-model failure, not an instruction or source deficiency. The repairs are bounded: add citations to approximately six paragraphs and one earlier paragraph. No new research is required."}ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;yRwfOJBr8OjDU4ZBLMIdGQVKiEujfXmZMDpi2kdTjvAƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx$ccaa93f7-9463-4e34-a693-1bf1507f08eeƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Dzi6mpOVkK8KQubcNhlwkzs-kmEMoipJ34X1-jihtMgƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkhtool_useƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;pcUxj3qXuw7YeTvvqtGCM8UTWZQ0z3Vy1jVgYbfslOkƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;0EMNDFPLN1C5MY_Ggrs8zmWqGV1LyxRlvVi9dqc4w7Eƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarklwriter-modelƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;5Im-dp_1_AY0qPEm0oJlZXb9M7Df0epWLyZV9Llj1Hsƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxqAdd missing inline citations to the final showdown section and Chicago Bulls paragraph, then resubmit for review.ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Au38iVOkdmOrOFQFTFpALsvIzNAUovNCvaRsjU8qJCIƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarky## Editorial Assessment â€” geometry-football-and-the-world-cup-final

### Deterministic standards
The automated gate returned `publishable: true` with one `warning` (attention-preserve-modality) flagging the description/summary for certainty language on an interpretive claim. That warning is addressed in repair item 3 below. No blocking gate failures are active.

### Source and assignment adequacy
The primary source (Conor Boland, The Conversation, 2026-07-17) is a well-evidenced academic analysis of Spain's passing geometry, with specific match events (Porro's 58th-minute goal, the MbappÃ© post-match quote via Reuters), player-tracking research citations, and the Chicago Bulls analogy â€” all material that can support the assigned article. Context 1 (Steve Georgakis, The Conversation, 2026-07-17) provides adequately specific claims about the two squads (goals conceded, scorers, Rodri's role, historical title context). The assignment is well-matched to the available evidence. No reporting-needed condition applies.

### Headline and lede
The headline "Spain's Geometry Meets Messi's Magic in World Cup Final Showdown" captures both analytical threads (Spain's geometric system, Argentina's Messi-centric structure) and adds "Showdown" for register without fabricating conflict not in the sources. It preserves the primary source's organizing terms. Acceptable.

The lede names the event concretely (who, where, what), cites both key sources before making factual claims, and correctly attributes the "fitting finale between the world's two highest-ranked teams" framing to Context 1 via the "according to" construction. Slightly awkward phrasing but not materially defective; the citation is visible.

### Body â€” first section and "The geometry of football triangles"
The first body paragraph accurately synthesizes the primary source: passing triangles, the choice-forcing mechanism, France's failure to close passing options, and MbappÃ©'s attributed quote. The MbappÃ© quotation is hyperlinked to the Reuters URL embedded within the primary source â€” the attribution chain is visible even if the manuscript cites the sub-link rather than the primary source itself. Acceptable.

**Material defect:** The Chicago Bulls/Phil Jackson paragraph contains four distinct factual claims originating in the primary source (Phil Jackson's 1987â€“1998 tenure, Tex Winter's triangle offence, the mechanics of placing three players on one side of the court, and the principle that triangles preserve alternatives). No inline citation appears in this paragraph. Under the article's own writing law â€” "every factual paragraph must make its reporting source unambiguous through an inline source link or an immediately continuing attribution" â€” this is a clear defect.

The remainder of this section (the probability-map discussion, the Porro/Olmo goal) is adequately tied to the primary source via the opening link.

### Body â€” "The geometry of the final showdown"
This section draws entirely on Context 1 for its factual substance: Spain's single goal conceded, the six-player scoring distribution, Messi's energy-conserving role, the "street fight" framing tied to Rodri, the 2010 title reference, and the six-decades claim on back-to-back championships. **None of these paragraphs carry an inline citation.** The attribution requirement is violated across the full section. This is the most material defect in the manuscript.

The closing paragraphs ("As these approaches collideâ€¦") are analytical extensions using appropriately hedged language ("could determine," "might allow"). They do not introduce invented facts. However, because the preceding paragraphs lack attribution, the analytical extension sits on an uncited factual base, compounding the problem.

### Modality and certainty
The description/summary phrase "they approach the contest with fundamentally different tactical philosophies" presents an interpretive characterization as settled fact without visible attribution, reproducing the deterministic gate's modality warning. Repairable by attaching attribution to Context 1 or hedging the phrasing.

### Fairness and harm
No allegations of wrongdoing, no named individuals criticized, no disputed claims requiring right-of-reply. No stereotyping, sensationalism, or commercial influence detected. Clean on fairness grounds.

### Pipeline diagnosis
